ton St., Constantine"]]},"m:L1190A":{"ti":"Constantine United Methodist Church","ins":[["Constantine United Methodist Church","Methodism began in Meek’s Mill (Constantine) in 1829 when the Reverend Erastus Felton came from the Ohio Methodist Episcopal Conference as a circuit rider to the St. Joseph Mission. This congregation, the earliest in Constantine, was organized in 1831 under the ministry of the Reverend Leonard Gurley. In 1848 the congregation erected a small brick church, which served until the present Gothic-style structure was built. Dedicated on December 1, 1878, the church was remodeled in 1955."]],"ph":{"u":"https://services3.arcgis.com/Jdnp1TjADvSDxMAX/ArcGIS/rest/services/Historical_Markers_Public_View/FeatureServer/0/509/attachments/3146?w=640","l":"https://gis-michigan.opendata.arcgis.com/maps/f3135a1337674b67b5af123eb41d6738","a":"Photo: Michigan History Center / DNR","lic":"Public record, no use restrictions","alt":"Wall-mount marker on a red brick wall next to red doors","src":"Michigan Historical Markers"},"f":[["Marker no.","L1190A"],["State Register","1984"],["Marker erected","1986"],["Era","Statehood Era (1815-1860)"],["Address","285 White Pigeon St, Constantine"]]},"n:80004070":{"pv":1,"d":"United States historic place","f":[["Listed","May 9, 1980"],["Significance","Architecture"],["Level","Local"],["Category","Building"],["Address","156 S. Washington St., Constantine"],["Built","1877"],["Style","Italianate architecture"]],"lk":[["Nomination file (National Archives)","https://catalog.archives.gov/id/25340533"],["Wikidata","https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q38251418"]],"ph":{"u":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/The_Art_Gallery_Building.jpg/960px-The_Art_Gallery_Building.jpg","l":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Art_Gallery_Building.jpg","a":"Jim Roberts","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licu":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","src":"Wikimedia Commons","alt":"The Art Gallery Building"},"wx":["The Art Galle